./docs/process_traces/2026-08-03-d111-backfill/coldgate_d100_bii/cold-ruling-2-nested-closure.md:26:The fence (`docs/process/state_kernel.json`, D100-BII-BINDING-01) reads: schema formulations may never bind **capture identity**, "telemetry rows carry no identity by construction; no third schema-shaped formulation may be proposed." Its grounds (D-106 grounds clause 4) are specific: one code path emits identical rows for measured and idle captures, so no per-file schema can distinguish *which occurrence* a file came from. That impossibility is about identity, and it is real — and it is untouched here: identity remains bound solely by interval containment and digest freeze, byte-identical per P6. Nested-content closure answers a different question — *is this content admission-shaped at all* — and that question is precisely what a writer-derived grammar CAN decide, because the producers are in-repo and their emitted set is closed. D-106 clause 3(c) itself orders a nested-content closure whose acceptance text is "unclassifiable means void," which is inherently an admission-grammar predicate. **Recorded distinction:** the fence bars schema-shaped formulations *offered as identity bindings*; the ruled shape is a content classifier whose soundness does not depend on rows carrying identity. And against the "isn't a grammar just a third enumeration?" reading: the trigger requires the fix be different *in kind* from the two failures — both failures enumerated the open forbidden complement with default-license; the grammar enumerates the closed admitted writer set with default-void. Inverted polarity over a closed set is the D-097-ratified pattern ("the reader's accepted set exactly equals the writer's emitted set"), not a third instance of the failed class.
./docs/process_traces/2026-08-03-d111-backfill/coldgate_d100_bii/cold-ruling-2-nested-closure.md:34:The ruled shape *implements* D-106 clause 3(c)'s own acceptance text ("unclassifiable means void") rather than altering what D-106 ordered, and per question 3 the fence does not reach it — so no amendment is required. Round 2 proceeds in the same commit arc: one commit, then a **fresh focused re-audit** of the nested-content surface against P1–P6 (fresh, not delta-only — two enumeration rounds mean the surface deserves a clean-eyes audit; delta verification suffices only for the P6 byte-identical components). Conditions: (a) this ruling is banked beside the packet and cited in the round-2 brief; (b) **return-to-decision trigger 1:** if grammar derivation finds a producer container that can be neither closed nor D-105-supersetted decidably, that is a D-106-level question — do not invent a bench formulation; (c) **return-to-gate trigger 2:** if the round-2 audit again finds *any* content licensed that the grammar should void, that is a third same-signature failure and returns here — there is no round 3 at the bench under any framing.
